target:
def Target : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Def1) (F : ArithmeticTheory) [F.RE] [𝗜𝚺₁ ⪯ F],
    Def5 K F → Def7 F ∉ K → Def4 K

b_raw:
{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The implication is reversed. Given K = F.theory, the claim uses consistency of F to conclude Def7 F ∉ K. The target assumes Def7 F ∉ K and concludes consistency of K, which under that equality is equivalent to consistency of F. This converse follows from explosion: an inconsistent F proves every sentence, so none could be absent from K."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The target specializes “sufficiently strong formal system” to a recursively enumerable arithmetic theory containing every theorem of IΣ₁, rather than covering other languages or other sufficient strength hypotheses."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def7 F uses the consistency sentence of the Craig presentation of F. This presentation has the same theorems as F, but its consistency sentence need not be syntactically identical to one formed from another presentation of F."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target preserves the exact identification of the mind’s conclusions with F’s theorems, but it makes the absence of the consistency sentence a premise instead of the conclusion. It therefore does not state item (c) or the second incompleteness implication."
}
